title: Proposition 33
---
order: 33
---
short_label: III.33
---
red_highlight: yes
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: On a given straight line to describe a segment of a circle admitting an angle equal to a given rectilinear angle.
----
proof:

Let *AB* be the given straight line, and the angle at *C* the given rectilinear angle.

It is required to describe on the given straight line *AB* a segment of a circle admitting an angle equal to the angle at *C.*

The angle at *C* is then acute, or right, or obtuse.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="330" height="240" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.33a diagram" src="propIII33a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.33a",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;210,30",
            "B;point;free;145,160",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "C1;point;free;80,60;0",
            "C2;point;free;100,100;0",
            "C3;point;free;30,100;0",
            "C12;line;connect;C1,C2",
            "C23;line;connect;C2,C3",
            "C;polygon;triangle;C1,C2,C3;black;0;0;0",
            "D';point;similar;B,A,C3,C2,C1;0;0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;D',A,140,30",
            "H;point;lineSlider;D',A,290,30;0",
            "DH;line;connect;D,H",
            "AE';line;perpendicular;A,D;0;0;0",
            "F;point;midpoint;A,B",
            "FG';line;perpendicular;F,B;0;0;0",
            "G;point;intersection;AE',FG'",
            "E;point;extend;A,G,A,G",
            "ABE;circle;radius;G,A;0;0;black;random",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "BG;line;connect;B,G",
            "FG;line;connect;F,G",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

First let it be acute as in the first figure.  Construct the angle *BAD* equal to the angle at *C* on the straight line *AB* and at the point *A.*  Therefore the angle *BAD* is also acute. [!just I.23]

Draw *AE* at right angles to *DA.*  Bisect *AB* at *F.* Draw *FG* from the point *F* at right angles to *AB,* and join *GB.* [!just I.10; I.12]

Then, since *AF* equals *FB,* and *FG* is common, the two sides *AF* and *FG* equal the two sides *BF* and *FG,* and the angle *AFG* equals the angle *BFG,* therefore the base *AG* equals the base *BG.* [!just I.4]

Therefore the circle described with center *G* and radius *GA* passes through *B* also.

Draw it as *ABE,* and join *EB.*

<div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII16/#cor">III.16,Cor.</a></div>
Now, since *AD* is drawn from A, the end of the diameter *AE,* at right angles to *AE,* therefore *AD* touches the circle *ABE.*

Since then a straight line *AD* touches the circle *ABE,* and from the point of contact at A a straight line *AB* has been drawn across in the circle *ABE,* the angle *DAB* equals the angle *AEB* in the alternate segment of the circle. [!just III.32]

But the angle *DAB* equals the angle at *C,* therefore the angle at *C* also equals the angle *AEB.*

Therefore on the given straight line *AB* the segment *AEB* of a circle has been described admitting the angle *AEB* equal to the given angle, the angle at *C.*

<hr width="30%">

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="320" height="240" tabindex="0"></canvas>
