</figure>

Next let the angle at *C* be right, and let it be again required to describe on *AB* a segment of a circle admitting an angle equal to the right angle at *C.*

Let the angle *BAD* be constructed equal to the right angle at *C,* as is the case in the second figure.  Bisect *AB* at *F.* Describe  the circle *AEB* with center *F* and radius either *FA* or *FB.* [!just I.23; I.10]

<div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII16/#cor">III.16 Cor.</a></div>
Therefore the straight line *AD* touches the circle *ABE,* because the angle at A is right.

And the angle *BAD* equals the angle in the segment *AEB,* for the latter too is itself a right angle, being an angle in a semicircle. [!just III.31]

But the angle *BAD* also equals the angle at *C,* therefore the angle *AEB* also equals the angle at *C.*

Therefore again the segment *AEB* of a circle has been described on *AB* admitting an angle equal to the angle at *C.*

<hr width="30%">

Next, let the angle at *C* be obtuse.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_2" width="320" height="240" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.33c diagram" src="propIII33c.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
