            "C1;point;free;90,100;0",
            "C2;point;free;40,100;0",
            "C3;point;free;20,60;0",
            "C12;line;connect;C1,C2",
            "C23;line;connect;C2,C3",
            "C;polygon;triangle;C1,C2,C3;black;0;0;0",
            "D';point;similar;B,A,C1,C2,C3;0;0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;D',A,280,40",
            "AD;line;connect;A,D",
            "AE';line;perpendicular;A,D;0;0;0",
            "F;point;midpoint;A,B",
            "FG';line;perpendicular;F,B;0;0;0",
            "G;point;intersection;AE',FG'",
            "E;point;extend;A,G,A,G",
            "ABE;circle;radius;G,A;0;0;black;random",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "BG;line;connect;B,G",
            "FG;line;connect;F,G",
            "H;point;circleSlider;ABE,155,55",
            "BH;line;connect;B,H",
            "AH;line;connect;A,H"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Construct the angle *BAD* equal to *C* on the straight line *AB* and at the point A as is the case in the third figure.   Draw *AE* at right angles to *AD.*  Bisect *AB* again at *F.*  Draw *FG* at right angles to *AB,* and join *GB.* [!just I.23; I.11, I.12]

Then, since *AF* again equals *FB,* and *FG* is common, the two sides *AF* and *FG* equal the two sides *BF* and *FG,* and the angle *AFG* equals the angle *BFG,* therefore the base *AG* equals the base *BG.* [!just I.4]

Therefore the circle described with center *G* and radius *GA* also passes through *B.*  Let it so pass, as *AEB.*

<div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII16/#cor">III.16 Cor.</a></div>
Now, since *AD* is drawn at right angles to the diameter *AE* from its end, *AD* touches the circle *AEB.*

And *AB* has been drawn across from the point of contact at A, therefore the angle *BAD* equals the angle constructed in the alternate segment *AHB* of the circle. [!just III.32]

But the angle *BAD* equals the angle at *C.*

Therefore the angle in the segment *AHB* also equals the angle at *C.*

Therefore on the given straight line *AB* the segment *AHB* of a circle has been described admitting an angle equal to the angle at *C.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
----
guide: This proposition is not used in the rest of the *Elements.*
